  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 156 PM CDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nAn approaching shortwave trough is forecast to eject off the High \nPlains of Colorado toward the upper Central Plains and Great Lakes. \nAs this occurs, a surface low will track eastward toward Iowa, \nbringing increasing chances for showers and thunderstorms starting \nThursday afternoon. The primary focus for strong to severe storms \nwill remain mostly west of the CWA; however, the far western \nperiphery of the CWA is in a Day 3 SPC Marginal Risk, with damaging \nwinds as the primary threat.\n\nWhile a Marginal Risk exists for severe weather, the paramount \nconcern for Thursday into late Friday is heavy rainfall. Tall, \nskinny CAPE profiles alongside PWs ranging from 2.00 to 2.50 inches \nfavor highly efficient warm-rain processes. In addition to these \nmoisture parameters, the slow-moving nature of the upper-level \ntrough will support several rounds of heavy rainfall through the \nfrontal passage late Friday. Given the elevated flood potential, the \nWPC has placed the area in Day 3 and Day 4 Slight Risk EROs.\n\nBehind the departing shortwave, surface high pressure builds into \nthe region for the weekend. Temperatures behind the front will run \nroughly 10 degrees cooler compared to forecast highs on Wednesday. \nAlthough model confidence degrades late in the weekend into early \nnext week, temperatures are expected to gradually rebound toward \nseasonal summertime values.\n\nAn approaching shortwave trough and surface low will bring \nincreasing chances for showers and thunderstorms Thursday afternoon \nthrough late Friday. Elevated PWs and tall, skinny CAPE profiles \nwill support multiple rounds of heavy rainfall, prompting Day 3 and \nDay 4 Slight Risk EROs from the WPC, alongside a Day 3 SPC Marginal \nRisk for severe storms along the western CWA periphery. Surface high \npressure brings drier and roughly 10 degrees cooler weather for the \nweekend before temperatures gradually rebound early next week.",
